{"id":"deferrals","ord":"a0","title":"Deferrals","desc":"NO DEFERRALS (the human, 2026-08-22): a PR ships everything its item names and everything it spots in touched code; what cannot be done STOPS and ASKS. The Inbox holds only what the human chooses to park. ONE exception: a test found flaky on master that a PR does not own is NOT a deferral — it goes to the Inbox's top-level `flaky tests` ledger, governed by dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY.","created":"2026-08-21T17:25:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:22:09-04:00"}
{"id":"flaky-tests","ord":"a7","title":"flaky tests — the ledger of tests found flaky on master that no PR owns","desc":"THE LEDGER, not the law: one entry per test file, sightings and dispositions as props on each entry. THE LAW lives in the boot chain — dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY (filed here, dispatched immediately, pi or grok never Opus, one PR when several are open, auto-merge on green). The CI watcher (roster: claude-ci) is the reporting channel; the orchestrator files what it reports.","created":"2026-08-22T11:51:06-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:21:35-04:00"}
{"id":"awaiting-word","ord":"a8","title":"Awaiting the human's word","desc":"The orchestrator's surfaced-but-unruled ledger, per orch-findings-inbox: each child names a proposal or observation and the word it awaits. Ruled children get their outcome written and are trashed or promoted to a lane.","created":"2026-08-29T11:40:15-04:00"}
{"id":"design-decisions","ord":"a9","title":"design decisions — agents' judgment calls awaiting the human's review","desc":"Choices agents made on their own, for your review — ONLY two kinds reach this queue (your ruling, 2026-08-29 20:15): (1) decisions with USER-FACING impact (what you see, how it behaves, where your data/config lives); (2) creation or non-creation of packages/*. Everything lower-level stays in the PR body's ## Design decisions section for reviewers and never lands here. One parent per PR, one child per qualifying choice, plain words. Reviewed: fine → trash; wrong → a lane to reverse it.","created":"2026-08-29T17:29:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T20:17:08-04:00"}
{"id":"deferral-437-phone","parent":"deferrals","ord":"a9","title":"olai#437: the flip's composition on a phone (two TARGET strips in one bar) — a look-to-answer","todo":true,"desc":"Accepted under the overnight mandate (minor, a genuine look-first question): how the filter bar composes on a phone now that it carries two touch-target strips. Look at it on your phone and rule.","created":"2026-08-29T22:07:26-04:00","custom":{"node":"done-visible-toggle","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/437"}}
{"id":"await-sitting-8","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a0","title":"The eighth sitting's conclusion awaits ratification (+ the recall-shape choice)","todo":true,"desc":"The consolidated conclusion sits at debates/lowy-electricity-2026-08-31/README.md (untracked until ratified; no PR exists — a debate, not a lane). Two words owed: (1) ratify — then it files to projects/olai/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-31.md and the roadmap absorbs the deepened roster design + the ledgered conditions; (2) the recall shape — #165 returns as plugin-recall with the contributed-matcher hook, or core-first with the hook population-gated, or decided at the PR's own review.","created":"2026-08-31T10:48:02-04:00","custom":{"node":"lowy-sitting-8"}}
{"id":"dd-449","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a0","title":"olai#449 (file delete): the page grows a Delete face beside Edit, with one confirming question","todo":true,"desc":"User-facing calls the author made, for your review: a document page (and an emptied outline's page) grows a Delete control beside Edit, behind one confirming question that names the path; the refusal for a non-empty outline names its records; git is the stated undo (no in-app inverse); delete is deliberately NOT in the MCP apply batch (single-op only, pinned by test). Fine → trash this entry; wrong → a lane reverses it.","created":"2026-08-31T13:08:34-04:00","custom":{"node":"no-file-delete-op","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/449"}}
{"id":"dd-453-dial","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a1","title":"olai#453: the kolu dial keeps its own package","todo":true,"desc":"The fold's first open question, ruled by the author with measurement rather than preference. `kolu-client` — the padi client, detect, `koluHalf` — stays its own package instead of folding into the plugin.\n\nTheir argument: the compiler today proves exactly one thing, that the dial names no olai package. A file-level sweep could match that in COVERAGE but not in KIND — every sweep needs a vacuity floor, and a resolution failure cannot be allowed to fail open. Folding it would also make `@olai/tests`' fake-padi import a general package importing a plugin, which costs either a fence carve-out or a fourth registry door. So: two packages per appliance, which the seventh lowy sitting called the honest end state and which odu already is — and `odu-client`'s placement comes out consistent rather than inconsistent.\n\nReaches you because it is a non-creation of a `packages/*`, which is the class the dag says you rule on. Review is asynchronous and never a merge gate: ratify and this is trashed, overturn and it becomes a lane.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"dd-453-interface","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a2","title":"olai#453: the plugin interface moves to packages/plugin-api","todo":true,"desc":"The fold's second open question. `@olai/plugins` becomes `@olai/plugin-api` at `packages/plugin-api/`, so `packages/plugins/` is purely the tenant container and the fence's graph walk stops reading as a contradiction. The tenants are `packages/plugins/olai-plugin-kolu` and `packages/plugins/olai-plugin-odu`, package names unscoped `olai-plugin-*` because the repo's one naming law is directory == package name.\n\nThe author argued this against the nested `packages/plugins/interface/` alternative in the PR body.\n\nReaches you because it CREATES a `packages/*`, which is the class the dag says you rule on. Asynchronous — never a merge gate.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"flake-deliveries","parent":"flaky-tests","ord":"a0","title":"packages/chat/src/deliveries.test.ts is flaky on master","todo":true,"desc":"Found in passing by #453's author and deliberately NOT fixed there (a fix smuggled into a packaging move is what the scope fence forbids). Their measurement: 9 runs on the fold's head gave 7 pass / 2 fail; 4 runs on master at 12b3b6ad gave 3 pass / 1 fail — same two cases, same stream error. So the flake predates the fold and no PR owns it, which is what puts it here.\n\n**The error string, from #454's author on 2026-09-01: `Controller is already closed`, under FULL-SUITE LOAD, green on re-run.** #455's author saw a one-off 4957/1 on a full run and lost the identity to a `tail -4`; three later full runs and six runs of the file alone were clean. CI's own `test` recipe has gone green at every head this week. So the shape is settled: **load-dependent, not random** — which is why it passes in isolation and in CI, and why a fix must be reproduced under load rather than by running the one file.\n\nWORTH THE HUMAN'S EYE BEYOND THE LEDGER: this is the DOORBELL's own delivery test, from #450 — the mechanism that wakes the orchestrator's conversation on fleet events. A flaky test over the waking path is the one flake that can hide a real fault in the thing that reports every other fault. So the lane's real question is not only \"why is the test flaky\" but \"is the test flaky, or is the delivery path racy under load\" — a controller closed twice can be a teardown racing a write, or a product that genuinely double-closes.\n\n**RULED BY THE HUMAN, 2026-09-01: grok authors, and this lane takes NO REVIEWER.** That makes CI the only gate between the author and master, which the brief states plainly to the author. Auto-merge on green, per the standing flake-class grant.\n\n**NOT YET DISPATCHED — the human called a hold at 09:52 after the brief was written.** Everything is prepared: brief, agent, tier, merge policy. Nothing is spawned and no step exists; the lane starts on their word.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T09:51:50-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-grok","brief":"briefs/flake-deliveries.md","merge":"merge-auto","node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453","tier":"mechanical"}}
{"id":"await-claude-weekly-limit","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a1","title":"Claude's weekly limit is at 94% with three Opus lanes queued","todo":true,"desc":"Read off the CI watcher's own banner when it spawned at 2026-09-01 03:40 UTC: **94% of the weekly limit used, resetting Sep 2 at 1am America/New_York** — about 25 hours out. Every Claude seat draws on it: the fold's author (Opus), the CI watcher (Sonnet), and every Opus reviewer.\n\n**Why it needs your word.** You ruled \"use claude for 3/5\", so three of the four queued lanes carry `agent-claude-opus` — the self-killing server, odu's lying `wait --settle`, and the adapter bump. At 94% with a full day before reset, those three are unlikely to all get through, and a lane that dies mid-implement leaves a half-written worktree rather than a clean stop.\n\n**What the orchestrator did NOT do:** reassign them. Your instruction named Claude, and the roster says your word beats the tier rule. The options, for you to pick at breakfast:\n1. Leave it — lanes stall at the limit and resume after the reset.\n2. Run ONE Opus lane (the self-killing server has the strongest Opus case: kernel truths tests cannot pin) and put the other two back on commodity authors.\n3. Put all three back on commodity authors with Opus reviewing, which is the tier rule's own default.\n\nThe fold itself is nearly clear of the limit — its remaining Claude work is the Sonnet CI watcher; its reviewers are grok and pi.","created":"2026-08-31T23:37:56-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"await-unsupervised-night","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a2","title":"A lane sat dead for six and a half hours and nothing noticed","todo":true,"desc":"**What happened.** The `mcp-mirrors-invisible` lane's author (pi, terminal 83892b49) died at 04:27 UTC on `Retry failed after 3 attempts: Connection error` — the kimi pool outage the roster names as one of pi's two known failure shapes. It had finished the implementation and a successful nix build; nothing was committed or pushed. The lane then sat dead until 10:57 UTC. Six and a half hours of the night produced nothing.\n\n**Why the doorbell did not save it.** It rang — every ten minutes, about forty times. The whole batch reached the conversation AT ONCE at 10:57 rather than as each was sent, which is the accumulate-and-deliver-on-reopen behaviour documented for a conversation whose session is not live. So the orchestrator did not ignore forty calls; it received one message containing forty. The distinction matters for diagnosis and not at all for the outcome.\n\n**The hole this exposes.** Every recovery path in the pipeline assumes the orchestrator is reachable when an event fires. When it is not, there is no floor: no agent notices its own death, no step times out, and a dispatched lane can burn a whole night silently. The heartbeat proves the WATCHER is alive; nothing proves the ORCHESTRATOR is consuming what it sends.\n\n**Not proposing a process change** — that is the human's to rule (the ban). What is worth their word: whether a lane that has been `doing` with an idle terminal for longer than some bound should be escalated by something other than the orchestrator, since the orchestrator is exactly the component that was missing.\n\n**Recovery, done:** a fresh pi (0117e874) resumed in the same worktree with a resume pointer, told to read the diff and judge it rather than trust it. The pool is back up; it is working.","created":"2026-09-01T06:58:33-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T06:58:33-04:00","custom":{"node":"mcp-mirrors-invisible"}}
{"id":"await-kimi-pool-out","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a3","title":"The kimi pool is out, so pi and opencode are both unavailable","todo":true,"desc":"Two pi sessions died on the same `Retry failed after 3 attempts: Connection error` about seven hours apart (04:27 and ~11:18 UTC), the second including on an explicit manual retry. Not the host — `api.github.com` answers 200 and gh works fine. Not the key — its expiry is ~2026-11-24 per the standing reminder. It is the Juspay pool, which pi and opencode SHARE, so BOTH commodity kimi seats are down until it returns.\n\n**What that leaves the fleet with:** grok, and Claude at 94% of its weekly limit until Sep 2 1am. That is one and a half usable authors, and the queue behind this lane is three lanes, three of them Opus-authored on the human's own instruction.\n\n**Already acted on standing authority, no ruling needed:** the mirrors lane moved to grok, which the choosing rule explicitly licenses when the pool is down. `lane-mi-review-a` was cancelled in the same beat, since an author never reviews its own lane and an ordinary-tier lane takes one claude-opus reviewer.\n\n**What needs the human:** whether to keep the remaining queue on the agents it names while two seats are dark, or re-plan it. This entry is the pool's status as of 2026-09-01 midday, and should be re-checked rather than trusted — an outage is exactly the kind of fact the board is not supposed to cache.","created":"2026-09-01T08:01:18-04:00","custom":{"node":"mcp-mirrors-invisible"}}
{"id":"await-monitor-hang","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a4","title":"A turn that armed a Monitor makes the next steer hang forever","todo":true,"desc":"Found by #456's author while bumping the adapter pin — nobody asked for it, and it costs you your instrument.\n\n**The fault.** claude-agent-acp issue #1039 (a steer after a QUEUED turn hangs that turn's prompt) has a SECOND trigger nobody knew about: a session in which a turn armed the **`Monitor`** tool leaves a later steered turn's `session/prompt` unanswered FOREVER. And the second trigger is worse than the first, because #366's guard does not cover it: with a Monitor, **nothing was ever queued**, so `queuedHere` stays `false`, so the panel still OFFERS the interrupt — and pressing it sits on *working…* until you press cancel.\n\n**Measured on the wire, not inferred.** Fresh sessions, four plain turns, a turn that spawned a subagent, and a turn that ran a background `Bash` all settle. A turn that armed a `Monitor` hangs — on 0.66.0, on 0.70.0, AND on a pristine 0.70.0 with our patches lifted off. So it is upstream's, older than this pin, and not something the bump introduced.\n\n**INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED by the reviewer (grok, 2026-09-01)**, which matters because this is a ruling rather than a fix. It confirmed the evidence shape and added the reason the Bash negative is not a lucky miss: the adapter does not special-case Monitor versus Bash at settlement — `liveBackgroundTasks` is shared — but one layer down **a Monitor wakes the model per event while a background Bash does not until it dies**. So widening the latch to \"any task was armed\" would take the interrupt away from conversations that never needed to lose it.\n\n**AND THE REVIEWER SHARPENED WHAT YOU ARE ACTUALLY RULING ON.** The repro waits for the arming turn's PROMPT to return and then steers — which leaves the TASK possibly still live. So the honest row is \"after an arming turn, with the task possibly still ticking\", NOT \"a Monitor once existed in this session, even after it died\". Those are two different guards, and the two places this is written down currently disagree about which: `docs/chat.md` reads as the live-watch case, `chat.ts` reads as session-permanent. **Whichever you rule, that disagreement wants closing** — and neither reading is a bump, so neither belongs in #456.","created":"2026-09-01T09:36:38-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T10:15:11-04:00","custom":{"node":"chat-adapter-bump","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/456"}}
{"id":"dd-457-picker","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a3","title":"olai#457: which files the wake picker offers","todo":true,"desc":"Reaches you because it changes what you SEE and press — the set of files the wake control offers.\n\n**The shape is better than the brief asked for.** Rather than filtering `.olai` at the picker, the PLUGIN now declares which of `@olai/format`'s file kinds its doorbell can watch (`PluginServerHalf.wake.kinds`, kolu declaring `[\"outline\"]`, typed against `FileKind`). That rides the roster and is compared against each path's kind in the browser — never a suffix match. The knowledge lives with the doorbell that has it.\n\n**Two exclusions, each with a different reason:**\n- **Trash and the leftover archives are not offered.** Not because they are meaningless — the opposite: unlike a `.md`, a walk over the trash really WOULD derive terminals, so the doorbell would ring about work somebody had put away.\n- **The `_olai/` mints are not offered** — Pins, Properties, Kolu, Inbox are outlines that will never carry a lane. Beyond those, every outline is offered, because which outline is a board is a thing the picker cannot know.\n\n**And one distinction worth your eye:** what is not OFFERED is not thereby REFUSED. A scope already stored on the trash keeps working, on the reasoning that curating a list is not a fault. If you disagree — if being off the list should mean being rejected — that is the thing to say, because the two are easy to conflate later.\n\nAsynchronous and never a merge gate: ratify and this is trashed, overturn and it becomes a lane.","created":"2026-09-01T10:21:56-04:00","custom":{"node":"wake-scope-outlines","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/457"}}
{"id":"flake-temp-store-race","parent":"flaky-tests","ord":"a1","title":"Two temp-store or git-remote race tests failed once, unidentified","todo":true,"desc":"**A PARTIAL ENTRY, filed deliberately rather than not at all.** #456's author saw 2 failures in one local run of the unit suite on 2026-09-01, both \"in the temp-store/git-remote race tests\". Two clean runs followed (4953/0 each). The round was comment-only — prose and docs, no behaviour — so the PR cannot have caused them.\n\n**The identity is gone.** The author summarised that run through `| tail -5`, so the test names and the error text scrolled away. It is not re-running to recover them, on the orchestrator's instruction: a re-run that happens to be green would prove nothing and would cost the honest record of an unreproduced sighting.\n\nSo this entry names a NEIGHBOURHOOD rather than a test, which is exactly what the one-entry-per-test-file rule is meant to prevent. It is here so a second sighting has something to match against — if someone sees two failures in the temp-store or git-remote race tests, this is the same animal and the two sightings together are worth more than either alone.\n\n**THE CAUSE OF THE MISSING NAME HAPPENED TWICE TODAY**, which makes it the more useful finding: #455's author lost a full-suite failure's identity to `| tail -4` in the morning, and #456's lost this one to `| tail -5` in the afternoon. Both authors reached the same fix afterwards without being told — capture the whole run to a file and summarise from the file (`bun test > run.log 2>&1; tail -5 run.log`), which costs nothing and keeps the names. Neither had the habit until one run too late.\n\nPutting that line in the standing dispatch brief is a PROCESS change and therefore the human's to approve, not the orchestrator's to write.","created":"2026-09-01T11:11:58-04:00","custom":{"node":"chat-adapter-bump","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/456"}}
